---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, CONTACT_EMAIL, LAST_UPDATED, REVIEW_CYCLE_MONTHS } from '../../data/site-config';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { displayDate } from '../../lib/format';
const lang = 'en';
---
<PageLayout title="Editorial policy: sources, testing and corrections" description="Where the public holidays, holiday eves and municipal school breaks come from, the order in which we read sources, how we test and what happens when we get it wrong." lang={lang} noindex={true} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Editorial policy' }]} showAuthor={false}>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>Editorial policy and corrections</h1>
    <p>A wrong holiday costs a leave day, a wrong school break a day of childcare. This page explains where every date comes from and what happens when one is wrong.</p>
    <h2>Responsibility</h2><p>Content and calculators are the responsibility of {AUTHOR_NAME}. Contact: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.</p>
    <h2>Order of sources</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Swedish statutes</strong> as published by the Riksdag: the Public Holidays Act (1989:253), the Annual Leave Act (1977:480), the Act on statutory time limits (1930:173), the Summer Time Ordinance (2001:127) and the Education Ordinance (2011:185);</li>
      <li><strong>Municipal decisions</strong>: each municipality's own page on term dates and school breaks;</li>
      <li><strong>Calculation rules</strong>: the Easter formula published by Germany's PTB and ISO 8601 week numbers.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Calendar sites, travel portals and newspaper articles are not sources. Where something is not officially fixed, such as a school's own study days or the treatment of Walpurgis Night at work, we say so plainly.</p>
    <h2>Computed, not copied</h2><p>No date on this site is typed by hand. An engine derives every holiday and school break from its rule, such as "the Saturday between 20 and 26 June" or "39 days after Easter Sunday". Automated tests compare Easter with a second formula over a thousand years and check bridge days, working days and breaks on every change.</p>
    <h2>Review cycle</h2><p>A full review at least every {REVIEW_CYCLE_MONTHS} months and after every amendment. New rules go in only once they are published in the Swedish Code of Statutes (SFS) or decided by the municipality.</p>
    <h2>When we get something wrong</h2>
    <ol><li>Check against the source.</li><li>Fix the rule in the engine and add a test.</li><li>Republish the corrected date on every page.</li></ol>
    <h2>Independence</h2><p>No money from travel firms, hotels or authorities. No affiliate links, no sponsored content, no lead-generation form.</p>
    <p>See also <a href={route('method', lang)}>method</a>. Version of {displayDate(LAST_UPDATED, 'en-GB')}.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
